export const INTRO_HTML = "<p class=\"post-lede\">Five hundred million years ago, evolution ran its most audacious experiment — producing every major animal body plan in roughly twenty million years. Nothing like it has happened since. Until, arguably, now. This series maps the current AI landscape through the lens of the Cambrian explosion, using the deepest pattern in the fossil record to identify what survives the next wave of decimation.</p>\n\n    <p class=\"drop-cap\">I was just about eight years old when I first stood before the <em>Anomalocaris</em>. It was at the National Museum of Natural History, in the cavernous hall filled with rocks and fossils and the musty odor of granite and gravel.</p>\n\n    <p>The <em>Anomalocaris</em> fossil was about one meter long, and a weird amalgamation of features — two grasping \"great appendages\" lined with tinier appendages; a thin, circular, pineapple-shaped mouth; compound eyes with over 16,000 lenses (similar to a dragonfly); a wing-like body. One could very well have mistaken them for prehistoric lobsters or aliens from James Cameron's Pandora, yet just over five hundred and twenty million years ago, these creatures ruled the seas as apex predators. All these evolutionary traits, even before the oceans had organisms with backbones (Paterson et al., 2011). I recall walking away from that display, confused — how had multi-lens eyes categorized oceanic lifeforms long before spinal construction ever did?!</p>\n\n    <p>Beyond the <em>Anomalocaris</em>, the exhibit featured other Burgess Shale oddities — <em>Hallucigenia</em>, an organism so mystifying that for the longest time, scientists had reconstructed its body upside down and backwards; <em>Opabinia</em>, with 5 eyes and a vacuum-cleaner-like nozzle for a face, a prehistoric Pokémon of sorts; <em>Wiwaxia</em>, a sort of armored slug whose shape was so polarizing it divided the scientific community; and little <em>Pikaia gracilens</em>, a flattened eel-like blob, two inches long, which happens to be one of the most iconic early chordates recorded (Conway Morris &amp; Caron, 2012). The Cambrian Evolution created some funky natural phenomena!</p>\n\n    <p>Those displays left a particular kind of mark on me. It wasn't \"wow, ancient biota are cool.\" It was closer to the realization that the forms life can take are not the ones I expected.</p>\n\n    <hr class=\"rule\"/>\n\n    <p>Fast forward to the present. I am sitting in Berkeley, an M.E.T. senior (since graduated 😄), trying to finish a final paper, surrounded by a laptop, three open browser tabs to <em>Nature</em>, <em>Cell</em>, and <em>PNAS</em> papers on bacterial warfare, a terminal running Claude Code, an open Cursor window, and an Opus 4.7 conversation on the best way to build a knowledge graph. On my other screen, Granola is quietly transcribing a YouTube video lecture on agentic workflows. There is an OpenClaw agent on my old MacBook Air, researching Bessemer and Felicis articles on my behalf for a blog I am drafting on BioLLMs. And on my phone: Perplexity News notifications on SpaceX and xAI's merger. On X, Jensen Huang is calling AI the largest infrastructure buildout in human history. I google \"AI Bubble 2026\" and get a wall of half-contradictory takes. And the word \"Cambrian\" — yes, the very same Cambrian — is used roughly eleven times a day in the Silicon Valley discourse.</p>\n\n    <p>And the thing is: they are not wrong!</p>\n\n    <blockquote class=\"pull\">\n      The structural parallel between what happened in the oceans roughly 539 to 515 million years ago and what we are seeing in the global technology stack in the modern era, specifically, in measurable ways, is the same pattern of events.\n    </blockquote>\n\n    <p>The analogy is genuinely useful. Not just metaphorically, the way every tech cycle gets called \"Darwinian\" or \"evolutionary\" because someone wants to tie their thesis to scientific buzzwords. Both are explosions of form. Both are preceded by long periods of hidden pre-adaptations. Both are catalyzed by a single, seemingly abstract innovation that unlocks dormant developmental toolkits. Both produce a staggering \"weird wonders\" prototypic phase, followed by \"species\" decimation. Both feature apex organisms that seem permanent in the short run, but can be threatened in the long run. And both culminate in a small number of durable traits that ultimately define downstream consequences.</p>\n\n    <p>I've contributed to investment memos on volatile startups, and observed in real-time Anthropic go from a $61.5B post-money in March 2025 to a $380B Series G in February 2026 — essentially a 6x increase in eleven months, in private markets, on a company that does not yet have public revenue that can justify it by any conventional SaaS multiple. Growth in AI has been exponential, and truly, the best way I have come to categorize it is as Cambrian.</p>\n\n    <p>This multi-part series is my attempt to contextualize the Cambrian analogy more formally, supported by both observations and evidence. Specifically, I break down the parallels across six ideological frames: speciation at hyperscale, transformers as the inflection point, proto-species and prototypes, org structures and organic hierarchies, evolutionary convergence, and lastly, Darwinian futures. Across each frame, the goal is to validate how biological lessons learned from the Cambrian Evolution could translate into successful navigation of the AI boom — being <em>prescriptive</em> about the present, <em>observational</em> about short- and medium-run trends, and <em>predictive</em> about the long run.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"callout\">\n      <div class=\"callout-tag\">A Quick Note on Framing</div>\n      <p>I am a biotechnologist by education, a VC operator by happy accident, and what follows is an exploration of how to categorize the shape of a rapidly shifting ecosystem. The ideas here are backed by papers I have explored, lectures I have sat through, and conversations I have been lucky to be part of — but they are equally speculative, offered in the spirit of thinking-out-loud rather than pronouncing-from-above.</p>\n      <p>Shoutouts to <strong>Ansh Chaurasia</strong> (technical validation), <strong>Shasi Pinnitti</strong> (business validation of AI valuations), and <strong>Dhruv Kulkarni</strong> (the conversation that catalyzed this).</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <div class=\"end-orn\">· · ·</div>";

export const S0_HTML = "<p class=\"post-lede\">As in any good computational case, the analogy is only useful if the base case is real. And the base case here lies in the history of the Cambrian Evolution. What follows is the biology — the mechanics of the explosion itself — and each pattern we uncover here will resurface, at digital speed, in the sections ahead.</p>\n\n    <!-- FIGURE 1: TIMELINE -->\n    <h3 class=\"sub\">The Deep-Time Stage</h3>\n    <div class=\"fig\">\n      <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n        <div class=\"fig-title\">The Deep-Time Stage</div>\n        <div class=\"fig-sub\">Geological Timeline — From the Ediacaran to Now</div>\n      </div>\n      <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n      <div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\">\n        <svg viewBox=\"0 0 960 168\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"\n     style=\"display:block;width:100%;max-width:100%;\"\n     font-family=\"DM Sans, system-ui, sans-serif\">\n\n  <rect width=\"960\" height=\"168\" fill=\"white\"/>\n\n  <!-- ══ ERA BLOCKS\n       Scale: 920px / 635 Ma ≈ 1.449 px/Ma  (x offset = 20)\n       Ediacaran  635–538.8  = 96.2 Ma  × 1.449 = 139px   x=20\n       Cambrian   538.8–487  = 51.8 Ma  × 1.449 = 75px    x=159\n       Ordovician 487–444    = 43 Ma    × 1.449 = 62px    x=234  (+4px breathing)\n       Paleozoic  444–252    = 192 Ma   × 1.449 = 278px   x=296\n       Mesozoic+  252–0      = 252 Ma   × 1.449 = 365px   x=574\n       Humans sliver at x=933\n  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->\n\n  <!-- Ediacaran -->\n  <rect x=\"20\"  y=\"16\" width=\"139\" height=\"52\" fill=\"#C4A882\"/>\n  <!-- Cambrian — blue -->\n  <rect x=\"159\" y=\"16\" width=\"75\"  height=\"52\" fill=\"#4f74ad\"/>\n  <!-- Ordovician — given 62px, text fits cleanly -->\n  <rect x=\"234\" y=\"16\" width=\"62\"  height=\"52\" fill=\"#4A7A5A\" opacity=\"0.85\"/>\n  <!-- Paleozoic continued -->\n  <rect x=\"296\" y=\"16\" width=\"278\" height=\"52\" fill=\"#8A7A6A\" opacity=\"0.72\"/>\n  <!-- Mesozoic + Cenozoic -->\n  <rect x=\"574\" y=\"16\" width=\"365\" height=\"52\" fill=\"#9B8A6A\" opacity=\"0.65\"/>\n  <!-- Humans sliver -->\n  <rect x=\"933\" y=\"16\" width=\"7\"   height=\"52\" fill=\"#4f74ad\"/>\n\n  <!-- Cambrian explosion glow -->\n  <rect x=\"157\" y=\"13\" width=\"28\" height=\"58\" fill=\"rgba(79, 116, 173,0.18)\" rx=\"0\"/>\n\n  <!-- ══ ERA TEXT — centered in each block ══ -->\n  <!-- Ediacaran: center = 20+69 = 89 -->\n  <text x=\"89\"  y=\"39\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"white\" font-size=\"10.5\" font-weight=\"700\" letter-spacing=\"1\">EDIACARAN</text>\n  <text x=\"89\"  y=\"55\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"rgba(255,255,255,0.75)\" font-size=\"8.5\">635–538.8 Ma</text>\n\n  <!-- Cambrian: center = 159+37 = 196 -->\n  <text x=\"196\" y=\"39\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"white\" font-size=\"10.5\" font-weight=\"700\" letter-spacing=\"0.5\">CAMBRIAN</text>\n  <text x=\"196\" y=\"55\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"rgba(255,255,255,0.82)\" font-size=\"8.5\">538–487</text>\n\n  <!-- Ordovician: center = 234+31 = 265 — 62px wide, text fits at font-size 9 -->\n  <text x=\"265\" y=\"39\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"white\" font-size=\"9\" font-weight=\"700\" letter-spacing=\"0.3\">ORDOVICIAN</text>\n  <text x=\"265\" y=\"55\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"rgba(255,255,255,0.75)\" font-size=\"8\">487–444</text>\n\n  <!-- Paleozoic: center = 296+139 = 435 -->\n  <text x=\"435\" y=\"39\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"white\" font-size=\"10.5\" font-weight=\"700\" letter-spacing=\"0.5\">PALEOZOIC (CONTINUED)</text>\n  <text x=\"435\" y=\"55\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"rgba(255,255,255,0.68)\" font-size=\"8.5\">444–252 Ma · Trilobites dominant</text>\n\n  <!-- Mesozoic: center = 574+182 = 756 -->\n  <text x=\"756\" y=\"39\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"white\" font-size=\"10.5\" font-weight=\"700\" letter-spacing=\"0.5\">MESOZOIC + CENOZOIC</text>\n  <text x=\"756\" y=\"55\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"rgba(255,255,255,0.68)\" font-size=\"8.5\">252 Ma – Present</text>\n\n  <!-- ══ EXPLOSION EMOJI ══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->\n  <text x=\"172\" y=\"84\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\">💥</text>\n\n  <!-- ══ END-PERMIAN — x=574 ════════════════════════════════════════ -->\n  <line x1=\"574\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"574\" y2=\"74\" stroke=\"#B04040\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,3\"/>\n  <text x=\"578\" y=\"83\"  fill=\"#B04040\" font-size=\"9\"   font-weight=\"700\">End-Permian</text>\n  <text x=\"578\" y=\"94\"  fill=\"#B04040\" font-size=\"8.5\">252 Ma · Trilobites extinct</text>\n\n  <!-- ══ HUMANS — right-aligned from x=930 ══════════════════════════ -->\n  <text x=\"928\" y=\"83\"  fill=\"#4f74ad\" font-size=\"9\"   font-weight=\"700\" text-anchor=\"end\">Humans</text>\n  <text x=\"928\" y=\"94\"  fill=\"#4f74ad\" font-size=\"8.5\" text-anchor=\"end\">~0.3 Ma</text>\n\n  <!-- ══ TRILOBITE REIGN BRACKET\n       521 Ma → x = 20 + (635-521)*1.449 = 20 + 165 = 185\n       252 Ma → x = 574\n  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->\n  <line x1=\"185\" y1=\"104\" x2=\"574\" y2=\"104\" stroke=\"#4f74ad\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <line x1=\"185\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"185\" y2=\"108\" stroke=\"#4f74ad\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <line x1=\"574\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"574\" y2=\"108\" stroke=\"#4f74ad\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"379\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#4f74ad\" font-size=\"9.5\" font-weight=\"700\">Trilobite reign: ~270 million years</text>\n\n  <!-- ══ CAMBRIAN EXPLOSION BRACKET\n       ~20 My: 538.8–518 → x=159 to x=159+29=188\n  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->\n  <line x1=\"159\" y1=\"126\" x2=\"188\" y2=\"126\" stroke=\"#6B5540\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <line x1=\"159\" y1=\"122\" x2=\"159\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#6B5540\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <line x1=\"188\" y1=\"122\" x2=\"188\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#6B5540\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"270\" y=\"130\" fill=\"#5A4A3A\" font-size=\"9\" font-weight=\"500\">~20 My explosion · ~20 phyla · No new phyla since</text>\n\n  <!-- ══ SCALE LABELS ═════════════════════════════════════════════ -->\n  <text x=\"20\"  y=\"152\" fill=\"#B0A898\" font-size=\"8.5\">635 Ma</text>\n  <text x=\"480\" y=\"152\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#C0B8B0\" font-size=\"8\">Source: ICS Chronostratigraphic Chart · Marshall 2006 · Erwin &amp; Valentine 2013</text>\n  <text x=\"940\" y=\"152\" text-anchor=\"end\"    fill=\"#B0A898\" font-size=\"8.5\">Present</text>\n\n</svg>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"fig-cap\">Proportional timeline from the Ediacaran through present. The Cambrian explosion (~538–518 Ma) fits within a ~20-million-year window yet produced essentially all modern animal body plans. Trilobites dominated for 270 million years — 13× the duration of the explosion itself, and longer than the dinosaurs' ~165 My reign.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <p>For about three billion years after life first appeared, Earth's oceans were a microbial \"mat\" — bacteria, archaea, eukaryotic single cells, maybe some simple multicellular experiments, all covering the ocean surface. Then, almost out of nowhere, something genuinely strange happened.</p>\n\n    <p>Between roughly 571 and 539 million years ago, in what is now labeled the Ediacaran period, a soft-bodied biota appeared: <em>Dickinsonia</em>, up to 1.4 meters of flat quilted pancake-like spheres; <em>Charnia</em>, fern-frond-looking species; <em>Kimberella</em>, possibly an early mollusk. Adolf Seilacher called them \"Vendobionta\" — a failed experiment in body-planning. The \"Garden of Ediacara\" had no predation, no mouths, no guts. A kind of ecological stagnancy.</p>\n\n    <p>But under the surface, the Ediacaran period meant something more. Under the guise of stagnancy, the ecosystem was slowly building pressure. And, around the 538.8 million-year mark — the Ediacaran-Cambrian boundary — the shift exploded: nearly every animal phylum — the topmost tier of animal classification, each defined by a fundamentally distinct body plan — alive today had made its fossil debut (Marshall, 2006; Erwin &amp; Valentine, 2013). Arthropods, chordates, mollusks, annelids, echinoderms — all of them.</p>\n\n    <!-- FIGURE 2: PHYLA COUNT -->\n    <h3 class=\"sub\">The Numbers That Make the Analogy Real</h3>\n    <div class=\"fig\">\n      <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n        <div class=\"fig-title\">Phyla Count — Cambrian vs. Total vs. AI Analog</div>\n        <div class=\"fig-sub\">How the explosion of biological body plans maps onto the AI model landscape</div>\n      </div>\n      <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n      <div class=\"comp\">\n        <div class=\"comp-bio\">\n          <div class=\"comp-side-lbl\">Biology</div>\n          <div class=\"bc-wrap\">\n            <div class=\"bc-row\">\n              <span class=\"bc-label\">Total phyla today</span>\n              <div class=\"bc-track\">\n                <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:100%;background:var(--fig-dark)\">\n                  <span>~35 animal phyla</span>\n                </div>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class=\"bc-row\">\n              <span class=\"bc-label\">Cambrian representatives</span>\n              <div class=\"bc-track\">\n                <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:57%;background:var(--blue)\">\n                  <span>~20 phyla (≈ half)</span>\n                </div>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class=\"bc-row\">\n              <span class=\"bc-label\">New phyla post-Cambrian</span>\n              <div class=\"bc-track\" style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;\">\n                <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:3%;background:var(--fig-mid)\"></div>\n                <span class=\"bc-zero\">0</span>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"comp-ai\">\n          <div class=\"comp-side-lbl\">AI Analog</div>\n          <div class=\"bc-wrap\">\n            <div class=\"bc-row\">\n              <span class=\"bc-label\" style=\"width:90px;\">LLMs 2017–2026</span>\n              <div class=\"bc-track\">\n                <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:100%;background:var(--blue)\">\n                  <span>194+ frontier models</span>\n                </div>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class=\"bc-row\">\n              <span class=\"bc-label\" style=\"width:90px;\">HuggingFace models</span>\n              <div class=\"bc-track\">\n                <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:100%;background:var(--blue-mid)\">\n                  <span>2.8M+ model artifacts</span>\n                </div>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class=\"bc-row\">\n              <span class=\"bc-label\" style=\"width:90px;\">Dominant arch.</span>\n              <div class=\"bc-track\">\n                <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:100%;background:var(--fig-dark)\">\n                  <span>Transformer (2017→)</span>\n                </div>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n          <p class=\"comp-note\">We mint frontier model variants faster than evolution minted phyla across the entire history of animal life.</p>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"fig-cap\">Sources: Marshall 2006; Erwin &amp; Valentine 2013; Zhang &amp; Shu 2021. AI: llm-timeline.com; Hugging Face model hub (May 2026). The \"~20 of ~35\" figure is a scholarly approximation — the defensible statement is \"roughly half of living animal phyla have fossilizable body plans appearing in the early Cambrian.\"</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- CREATURE CARDS -->\n    <h3 class=\"sub\">The Weird Wonders of the Burgess Shale</h3>\n    <p>Charles Marshall's phrasing from <em>Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences</em>: \"the geologically abrupt appearance of fossils representing quite disparate body plans of many living metazoan phyla.\" But what that sentence obscures is just how <em>genuinely strange</em> those body plans were.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"fig\">\n      <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n        <div class=\"fig-title\">Burgess Shale — Six Specimens from the Weird Wonders</div>\n        <div class=\"fig-sub\">~505 Ma · British Columbia · SVG reconstructions</div>\n      </div>\n      <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n      <div class=\"creature-grid\">\n        <div class=\"cc\">\n          <div class=\"cc-name\">Anomalocaris</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-tag\">Apex Predator <span class=\"p\">|</span> Radiodont</div>\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 190 56\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n            <ellipse cx=\"94\" cy=\"30\" rx=\"62\" ry=\"13\" fill=\"#4f74ad\" opacity=\"0.12\"/>\n            <ellipse cx=\"94\" cy=\"29\" rx=\"54\" ry=\"10\" fill=\"#4f74ad\" opacity=\"0.28\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"156\" cy=\"27\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#4f74ad\" opacity=\"0.65\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"156\" cy=\"27\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#345583\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"154\" cy=\"25\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"white\"/>\n            <path d=\"M148 23 Q137 11 123 17\" stroke=\"#4f74ad\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"/>\n            <path d=\"M148 32 Q137 43 123 37\" stroke=\"#4f74ad\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"/>\n            <path d=\"M123 24 Q113 15 103 16 Q93 17 83 15 Q73 13 63 17 Q53 21 43 23\" stroke=\"#89a7cc\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-dasharray=\"3,2\"/>\n            <path d=\"M123 35 Q113 42 103 40 Q93 38 83 40 Q73 42 63 38 Q53 34 43 34\" stroke=\"#89a7cc\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-dasharray=\"3,2\"/>\n            <path d=\"M29 29 Q16 22 12 29 Q16 36 29 29\" fill=\"#4f74ad\" opacity=\"0.5\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"159\" cy=\"29\" r=\"5\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>\n          </svg>\n          <div class=\"cc-facts\">Grasping frontal appendages · Circular \"pineapple\" mouth · <strong>16,000+ lenses</strong> per compound eye · Wing-like lateral lobes</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-size\">Up to ~1m <span class=\"p\">|</span> Apex predator <span class=\"p\">|</span> Extinct by Ordovician</div>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"cc\">\n          <div class=\"cc-name\">Hallucigenia</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-tag\">Lobopodian <span class=\"p\">|</span> Ecdysozoan</div>\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 190 56\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n            <rect x=\"36\" y=\"25\" width=\"116\" height=\"10\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#8B6B4A\" opacity=\"0.7\"/>\n            <line x1=\"51\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"48\" y2=\"12\" stroke=\"#6B4B2A\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <line x1=\"64\" y1=\"24\" x2=\"61\" y2=\"11\" stroke=\"#6B4B2A\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <line x1=\"77\" y1=\"23\" x2=\"74\" y2=\"10\" stroke=\"#6B4B2A\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <line x1=\"90\" y1=\"23\" x2=\"87\" y2=\"9\" stroke=\"#6B4B2A\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <line x1=\"103\" y1=\"23\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"9\" stroke=\"#6B4B2A\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <line x1=\"116\" y1=\"23\" x2=\"113\" y2=\"10\" stroke=\"#6B4B2A\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <line x1=\"129\" y1=\"24\" x2=\"126\" y2=\"11\" stroke=\"#6B4B2A\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <line x1=\"51\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"48\" y2=\"46\" stroke=\"#8B6B4A\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n            <line x1=\"64\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"61\" y2=\"46\" stroke=\"#8B6B4A\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n            <line x1=\"77\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"74\" y2=\"46\" stroke=\"#8B6B4A\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n            <line x1=\"90\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"87\" y2=\"46\" stroke=\"#8B6B4A\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n            <line x1=\"103\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"46\" stroke=\"#8B6B4A\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n            <line x1=\"116\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"113\" y2=\"46\" stroke=\"#8B6B4A\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"152\" cy=\"30\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#8B6B4A\" opacity=\"0.8\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"154\" cy=\"28\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#5A3A1A\"/>\n          </svg>\n          <div class=\"cc-facts\">7–8 pairs of clawed legs · Defensive dorsal spines · <strong>Reconstructed upside-down for decades</strong> · Ancestor of velvet worms</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-size\">0.5–3.5 cm <span class=\"p\">|</span> ~505 mya <span class=\"p\">|</span> Corrected 1991 + 2015</div>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"cc\">\n          <div class=\"cc-name\">Opabinia</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-tag\">Stem arthropod <span class=\"p\">|</span> Five eyes</div>\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 190 56\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n            <rect x=\"38\" y=\"21\" width=\"100\" height=\"17\" rx=\"8.5\" fill=\"#5B8FA8\" opacity=\"0.5\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"141\" cy=\"17\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#4A7A9A\" opacity=\"0.8\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"15\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#4A7A9A\" opacity=\"0.8\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"158\" cy=\"17\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#4A7A9A\" opacity=\"0.8\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"162\" cy=\"24\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#4A7A9A\" opacity=\"0.6\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"159\" cy=\"31\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#4A7A9A\" opacity=\"0.5\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"141\" cy=\"17\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1A3A5A\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"15\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#1A3A5A\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"158\" cy=\"17\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#1A3A5A\"/>\n            <path d=\"M137 30 Q150 39 163 44\" stroke=\"#3A6A8A\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"165\" cy=\"45\" r=\"2.8\" fill=\"#4A7A9A\" opacity=\"0.7\"/>\n            <path d=\"M38 25 Q25 18 19 26 Q25 34 38 33\" fill=\"#5B8FA8\" opacity=\"0.45\"/>\n          </svg>\n          <div class=\"cc-facts\"><strong>Five stalked eyes</strong> · Flexible clawed proboscis · Fan-shaped tail · Made the Paleontological Association audibly laugh when described</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-size\">4–7 cm <span class=\"p\">|</span> ~505 mya <span class=\"p\">|</span> Fewer than 20 good specimens</div>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"cc\">\n          <div class=\"cc-name\">Pikaia gracilens</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-tag\">Earliest chordate <span class=\"p\">|</span> Our ancestor</div>\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 190 56\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n            <path d=\"M26 31 Q46 16 78 26 Q110 36 140 20 Q160 12 170 27\" stroke=\"#4A7A5A\" stroke-width=\"6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\" opacity=\"0.52\"/>\n            <path d=\"M30 30 Q50 17 82 25 Q114 34 142 21\" stroke=\"white\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,2\" fill=\"none\" opacity=\"0.85\"/>\n            <line x1=\"43\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"46\" y2=\"36\" stroke=\"#3A6A4A\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <line x1=\"57\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"34\" stroke=\"#3A6A4A\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <line x1=\"71\" y1=\"21\" x2=\"74\" y2=\"35\" stroke=\"#3A6A4A\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <line x1=\"85\" y1=\"24\" x2=\"88\" y2=\"38\" stroke=\"#3A6A4A\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <line x1=\"99\" y1=\"27\" x2=\"102\" y2=\"41\" stroke=\"#3A6A4A\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <line x1=\"113\" y1=\"24\" x2=\"116\" y2=\"36\" stroke=\"#3A6A4A\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <ellipse cx=\"169\" cy=\"27\" rx=\"8\" ry=\"5.5\" fill=\"#4A7A5A\" opacity=\"0.8\"/>\n            <ellipse cx=\"26\" cy=\"31\" rx=\"5.5\" ry=\"4.5\" fill=\"#4A7A5A\" opacity=\"0.6\"/>\n          </svg>\n          <div class=\"cc-facts\">Chevron myomeres · Notochord-like dorsal rod · <strong>Ancestor of all vertebrates</strong> · Survived when Anomalocaris did not</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-size\">~4–5 cm <span class=\"p\">|</span> ~508–505 mya <span class=\"p\">|</span> 100+ specimens known</div>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"cc\">\n          <div class=\"cc-name\">Wiwaxia</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-tag\">Armored slug <span class=\"p\">|</span> Sclerite-bearer</div>\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 190 56\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n            <ellipse cx=\"95\" cy=\"36\" rx=\"50\" ry=\"13\" fill=\"#9B7A4A\" opacity=\"0.38\"/>\n            <ellipse cx=\"95\" cy=\"35\" rx=\"46\" ry=\"11\" fill=\"#9B7A4A\" opacity=\"0.62\"/>\n            <ellipse cx=\"67\" cy=\"28\" rx=\"7\" ry=\"4\" fill=\"#7A5A2A\" opacity=\"0.8\" transform=\"rotate(-15,67,28)\"/>\n            <ellipse cx=\"80\" cy=\"26\" rx=\"7\" ry=\"4\" fill=\"#7A5A2A\" opacity=\"0.8\" transform=\"rotate(-8,80,26)\"/>\n            <ellipse cx=\"93\" cy=\"25\" rx=\"7\" ry=\"4\" fill=\"#7A5A2A\" opacity=\"0.8\"/>\n            <ellipse cx=\"106\" cy=\"26\" rx=\"7\" ry=\"4\" fill=\"#7A5A2A\" opacity=\"0.8\" transform=\"rotate(8,106,26)\"/>\n            <ellipse cx=\"119\" cy=\"28\" rx=\"7\" ry=\"4\" fill=\"#7A5A2A\" opacity=\"0.8\" transform=\"rotate(15,119,28)\"/>\n            <line x1=\"73\" y1=\"24\" x2=\"67\" y2=\"9\" stroke=\"#6B4B2A\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <line x1=\"85\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"81\" y2=\"7\" stroke=\"#6B4B2A\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <line x1=\"95\" y1=\"21\" x2=\"95\" y2=\"6\" stroke=\"#6B4B2A\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <line x1=\"105\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"109\" y2=\"7\" stroke=\"#6B4B2A\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <line x1=\"117\" y1=\"24\" x2=\"123\" y2=\"9\" stroke=\"#6B4B2A\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n          </svg>\n          <div class=\"cc-facts\">Overlapping dorsal sclerites · Defensive spines · Soft muscular foot · <strong>Divided the scientific community</strong> on its classification for decades</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-size\">Up to ~5 cm <span class=\"p\">|</span> ~505 mya <span class=\"p\">|</span> Mollusc/annelid affinity debated</div>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"cc\" style=\"border-top-color:var(--charcoal);\">\n          <div class=\"cc-name\">Trilobites</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-tag\" style=\"color:var(--mid);\">270-Million-Year Dynasty</div>\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 190 56\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><defs><clipPath id=\"tb\"><ellipse cx=\"95\" cy=\"28\" rx=\"93\" ry=\"26\"/></clipPath></defs><ellipse cx=\"95\" cy=\"28\" rx=\"93\" ry=\"26\" fill=\"#4f74ad\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><g clip-path=\"url(#tb)\"><path d=\"M2 28 Q8 4 30 6 L30 50 Q8 52 2 28Z\" fill=\"#4f74ad\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"28\" y2=\"10\" stroke=\"rgba(255,255,255,0.25)\" stroke-width=\"0.7\"/><line x1=\"8\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"28\" y2=\"16\" stroke=\"rgba(255,255,255,0.25)\" stroke-width=\"0.7\"/><line x1=\"8\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"28\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"rgba(255,255,255,0.25)\" stroke-width=\"0.7\"/><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"28\" y2=\"46\" stroke=\"rgba(255,255,255,0.25)\" stroke-width=\"0.7\"/><path d=\"M30 6 L30 50\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><g stroke=\"rgba(255,255,255,0.22)\" stroke-width=\"0.6\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M35 5 Q36 28 35 51\"/><path d=\"M47 3 Q48 28 47 53\"/><path d=\"M59 2.5 Q60 28 59 53.5\"/><path d=\"M71 2.8 Q72 28 71 53.2\"/><path d=\"M83 3.5 Q84 28 83 52.5\"/><path d=\"M95 4.5 Q96 28 95 51.5\"/><path d=\"M107 5.5 Q108 28 107 50.5\"/><path d=\"M119 7 Q120 28 119 49\"/><path d=\"M131 9 Q132 28 131 47\"/></g><g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"><path d=\"M35 5 C38 3 44 2.2 47 3\"/><path d=\"M35 51 C38 53 44 53.8 47 53\"/><path d=\"M47 3 C50 1.5 56 1.2 59 2.5\"/><path d=\"M47 53 C50 54.5 56 54.8 59 53.5\"/><path d=\"M59 2.5 C62 1.5 68 1.5 71 2.8\"/><path d=\"M59 53.5 C62 54.5 68 54.5 71 53.2\"/><path d=\"M71 2.8 C74 2 80 2 83 3.5\"/><path d=\"M71 53.2 C74 54 80 54 83 52.5\"/><path d=\"M83 3.5 C86 3 92 3 95 4.5\"/><path d=\"M83 52.5 C86 53 92 53 95 51.5\"/><path d=\"M95 4.5 C98 3.5 104 3.5 107 5.5\"/><path d=\"M95 51.5 C98 52.5 104 52.5 107 50.5\"/><path d=\"M107 5.5 C110 4.5 116 5 119 7\"/><path d=\"M107 50.5 C110 51.5 116 51 119 49\"/><path d=\"M119 7 C122 6 128 7 131 9\"/><path d=\"M119 49 C122 50 128 49 131 47\"/></g><rect x=\"82\" y=\"22\" width=\"56\" height=\"12\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#345583\" opacity=\"0.5\"/><path d=\"M131 9 Q140 4 155 3.5 Q172 3 182 8 Q190 14 188 28 Q190 42 182 48 Q172 53 155 52.5 Q140 52 131 47 Z\" fill=\"#4f74ad\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><path d=\"M145 12 Q155 8 165 10 Q172 13 174 20 Q175 28 174 36 Q172 43 165 46 Q155 48 145 44 Q140 38 139 28 Q140 18 145 12Z\" fill=\"#345583\" opacity=\"0.65\"/><ellipse cx=\"160\" cy=\"17\" rx=\"7\" ry=\"4.5\" fill=\"rgba(255,255,255,0.2)\" transform=\"rotate(-15 160 17)\"/><ellipse cx=\"160\" cy=\"39\" rx=\"7\" ry=\"4.5\" fill=\"rgba(255,255,255,0.2)\" transform=\"rotate(15 160 39)\"/><circle cx=\"162\" cy=\"17\" r=\"1.5\" fill=\"#1a2a40\" opacity=\"0.7\"/><circle cx=\"162\" cy=\"39\" r=\"1.5\" fill=\"#1a2a40\" opacity=\"0.7\"/><path d=\"M155 15 Q158 13 163 14\" stroke=\"rgba(255,255,255,0.3)\" stroke-width=\"0.5\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M155 41 Q158 43 163 42\" stroke=\"rgba(255,255,255,0.3)\" stroke-width=\"0.5\" fill=\"none\"/></g></svg>\n          <div class=\"cc-facts\">Calcite-reinforced exoskeleton · Three transverse regions · <strong>22,000+ described species</strong> · Reigned 270 million years — longer than the dinosaurs</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-size\">1mm–72cm <span class=\"p\">|</span> ~521–252 mya <span class=\"p\">|</span> Extinct at end-Permian</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- CATALYST CARDS -->\n    <h3 class=\"sub\">Three Catalysts — The Cambrian's Ignition Sequence</h3>\n    <p>The cause of the Cambrian explosion is still debated. Modern consensus points to a three-part ignition sequence — three mutually reinforcing forces that collectively unlocked body-plan diversity. Each has a direct AI parallel.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"fig\">\n      <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n        <div class=\"fig-title\">The Ignition Sequence</div>\n        <div class=\"fig-sub\">Three catalysts that unlocked the Cambrian body-plan explosion</div>\n      </div>\n      <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n      <div class=\"cat-grid\">\n        <div class=\"cat-card\">\n          <span class=\"cat-n\">01 / 03</span>\n          <div class=\"cat-title\">Oxygenation</div>\n          <div class=\"cat-body\">Atmospheric O₂ rose from ~1–10% PAL to near-modern levels — \"modern-like ocean oxygenation at ~521 Ma for the first time in Earth history\" (Chen et al. 2015, <em>Nature Communications</em>). Macroscopic predation became metabolically sustainable.</div>\n          <span class=\"cat-stat\">~521 Ma → Modern O₂</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"cat-card\">\n          <span class=\"cat-n\">02 / 03</span>\n          <div class=\"cat-title\">Developmental Toolkits</div>\n          <div class=\"cat-body\">Hox gene clusters had been quietly assembling for tens of millions of years — latent, waiting for a permissive environment. Erwin et al. (2011) called it \"macroevolutionary lag.\" Hox genes scale from 0 in sponges to 39 in humans.</div>\n          <span class=\"cat-stat\">0 → 39 Hox genes</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"cat-card\">\n          <span class=\"cat-n\">03 / 03</span>\n          <div class=\"cat-title\">Ecological Feedback</div>\n          <div class=\"cat-body\">Once predators appeared, prey evolved biomineralized shells. Shell-crushing predators followed. Burrowing defenses emerged. Bottjer et al. (2000): the Cambrian Substrate Revolution — matgrounds became mixgrounds. The Proterozoic way of life became physically impossible.</div>\n          <span class=\"cat-stat\">Matgrounds → Mixgrounds</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <blockquote class=\"pull\">\n      \"The geologically abrupt appearance of fossils representing quite disparate body plans of many living metazoan phyla.\"\n      <cite>— Charles Marshall, <em>Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences</em>, 2006</cite>\n    </blockquote>\n\n    <p>If that sequence feels familiar, it should. A dormant capability was there all along — then a threshold was unlocked, and a mad race of coevolution filled every niche. Exactly what we observe with technological lifecycles, and most recently, with the AI boom.</p>\n\n    <p>But here's the part that always gets me. During the Cambrian, evolution was clearly not trying every possible body plan. It was combinatorial exploration — but within the constraints of the developmental toolkit. And within those constraints, the winners were deeply contingent. <em>Pikaia gracilens</em>, a little four-centimeter flattened worm, was not obviously better than anything else in the Burgess Shale. But its lineage made it. The <em>Anomalocaris</em>, with their 16,000-lens eyes and meter-long bodies, did not. And the trilobites, the great Cambrian success story, would dominate for 270 million years before blinking out in the Permian.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"callout\">\n      <div class=\"callout-tag\">The Analogy's Payload</div>\n      <p>A phylum-level innovation (the notochord), assembled during a permissive developmental moment (the Cambrian), from a quiet little ancestor (<em>Pikaia</em>), can outlast and outscale all the apex predators of its own era. That is exactly what I want to argue is happening in AI right now — and it is almost never the thing people are optimizing for.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <div class=\"end-orn\">· · ·</div>";

export const S1_BEFORE_YC = "<p class=\"post-lede\">How fast are new forms appearing? And why? The Cambrian explosion produced roughly 20 animal phyla in about 20 million years. No new phyla have appeared in the half-billion years since. Hold that number as you read.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"stat-grid\">\n      <div class=\"stat-box\">\n        <span class=\"stat-val\">52.5%</span>\n        <span class=\"stat-label\">of global VC captured by AI in 2025 — first majority year (PitchBook)</span>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"stat-box\">\n        <span class=\"stat-val\">1 / 3 days</span>\n        <span class=\"stat-label\">average new frontier AI model release cadence (AI Flash Report, Jun '26)</span>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"stat-box\">\n        <span class=\"stat-val\">$675B+</span>\n        <span class=\"stat-label\">Big Five hyperscaler capex committed for 2026 alone (Futurum)</span>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"stat-box\">\n        <span class=\"stat-val\">2.8M+</span>\n        <span class=\"stat-label\">open models on Hugging Face by 2026 — the 2nd million arrived ~65% faster than the 1st</span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <p class=\"lead drop-cap\">The Cambrian explosion produced roughly 20 animal phyla in about 20 million years. No new phyla have appeared in the half-billion years since. Hold that number as you read the data below, because the AI analog is absurd on its face — species proliferating by the thousand per year, at a tempo measured in weeks rather than millennia.</p>\n\n    <p>But I want to flag the through-line of this entire section upfront, because it is the part almost everyone gets wrong. Lightspeed has written about a \"Cambrian explosion of models.\" Bessemer calls it the \"AI Big Bang.\" a16z talks about value accruing across every layer. Every one of these framings narrates the <em>explosion</em> — the part where life gets weird and abundant — and then stops. But the actual Cambrian record has a second act that never makes it onto the slide: <strong>decimation</strong>. Disparity peaks early, and then most of it gets pruned, hard. If you only tell the first half, you are not using the analogy. You are using it as a vibe. This section is my attempt to tell both halves, with the data for each.</p>\n\n    <h3 class=\"sub\">Pillar 1 — Capital Markets: The Sedimentary Layer</h3>\n    <p>In the biological Cambrian, the sea floor was built from mineral-rich sediment — accumulated over hundreds of millions of years, unlocking the raw material for biomineralization and the first hard bodies. In the AI Cambrian, capital is that sediment. It is the foundation that everything else precipitates from, and it has been accumulating at a rate that makes the Cambrian look slow.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"fig\">\n      <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n        <div class=\"fig-title\">AI Share of Global Venture Capital</div>\n        <div class=\"fig-sub\">2022–2025 · Percentage of total global VC deployed to AI companies</div>\n      </div>\n      <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n      <div class=\"bc-wrap\">\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">2022</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\">\n            <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:15%;background:var(--fig-mid)\"><span>~15%</span></div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">2023</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\">\n            <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:25%;background:var(--fig-mid)\"><span>25%</span></div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">2024</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\">\n            <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:37%;background:var(--blue-light)\"><span>~37%</span></div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">2025</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\">\n            <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:52.5%;background:var(--blue)\"><span>52.5% — first majority year</span></div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"fig-cap\">Source: PitchBook Q4 2025 Global VC First Look; CB Insights State of AI 2025. PitchBook: $192.7B of $366.8B total. CB Insights: $225.8B — gap reflects classification methodology differences. Both agree AI crossed 50%.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <p>In 2025, AI captured 52.5% of all global venture capital — $192.7B of $366.8B per PitchBook, the first time any single technology category has claimed a majority of venture dollars in one year. (CB Insights, using a wider definition of \"AI,\" puts the number at $225.8B; the share is roughly the same, the denominator is what differs.) The top rounds alone tell the concentration story. OpenAI raised $40B at a $300B post-money. Anthropic stacked a $13B Series F, a $30B Series G, and a $65B Series H in under a year, closing at a $965B valuation that — as of this writing — makes it the most valuable private company on Earth.</p>\n\n    <p>Then there are the specimens that exist purely as capital. Thinking Machines Lab raised a $2B seed at a $12B valuation with no product and barely twenty employees. Safe Superintelligence raised $2B at $32B on the same terms — a body plan funded before it had a body. This is speciation of capital, not of revenue, and it is exactly the kind of thing that happens when the resource barrel is overflowing.</p>\n\n    <p>And here is the part the headline number hides. Only 823 VC funds raised capital in 2025, down from 4,430 in 2022 — an 81% collapse in fund count. PitchBook's Kyle Stanford put it about as bluntly as an analyst can: <em>\"You're in AI, or you're not.\"</em> The capital is not spreading. It is pooling at the very top of the trophic chain while the entire lower ecosystem starves. That is not just a lot of money flowing to one category. That is maximum disparity — the resource barrel filling for a handful of apex organisms while the floor empties out beneath them.</p>\n\n    <h3 class=\"sub\">The YC Signal — Where the Next Generation of Founders Is Going</h3>\n\n    <div class=\"fig\">\n      <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n        <div class=\"fig-title\">YC AI Share by Batch</div>\n        <div class=\"fig-sub\">W24 through F25 · Percentage of batch companies incorporating AI</div>\n      </div>\n      <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n      <div class=\"bc-wrap\">\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">W24</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\">\n            <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:50%;background:var(--fig-mid)\"><span>~50%</span></div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">S24</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\">\n            <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:67%;background:var(--blue-light)\"><span>~67%</span></div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">X25 (Spring)</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\">\n            <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:47%;background:var(--blue-light)\"><span>47% explicit agents (67/144)</span></div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">F25 (Fall)</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\">\n            <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:92%;background:var(--blue)\"><span>92% — baseline, not differentiator</span></div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"fig-cap\">Source: CB Insights; YC batch analysis. By F25, AI had stopped being a differentiator and become the baseline. YC now runs 4 batches per year.</p>\n    </div>";

export const S1_AFTER_YC = "<p>What makes YC the cleanest signal is that it is a leading indicator of where the next generation of founders thinks the money is — and right now they think it is entirely here. Garry Tan has claimed the Fall 2025 batch grew revenue roughly 10% week over week, with founders closing six-figure enterprise contracts inside the three-month program. Treat that number with the skepticism it deserves: it comes from the accelerator's own CEO, the metric is conveniently unspecified, and he has made a version of the same claim for several batches running. But the directional signal is real — YC's public call is now for the first one-person, billion-dollar company. The thing to notice biologically is that 92% AI-native does not mean 92% differentiated. When every organism in the batch shares the same body plan, \"has AI\" stops being an adaptation and becomes the water they all swim in.</p>\n\n    <h3 class=\"sub\">Pillar 2 — Across the Stack: The CaaS Taxonomy</h3>\n    <p>To talk about the AI stack rigorously, you need a taxonomy. The best one I've found is Navin Chaddha's at Mayfield Fund: <em>Cognition-as-a-Service</em> (CaaS). It maps to the biological framing almost suspiciously well.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"fig\">\n      <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n        <div class=\"fig-title\">The CaaS Stack — Mapped to Cambrian Biology</div>\n        <div class=\"fig-sub\">Cognition-as-a-Service framework · Mayfield Fund · Biological analogs: author's framing</div>\n      </div>\n      <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n      <div class=\"funnel\">\n        <div class=\"f-layer\" style=\"background:#2C2522;border-radius:5px 5px 0 0;\"><span class=\"f-num\">6</span><div class=\"f-body\"><div class=\"f-title\">Semis &amp; Cognitive Plumbing</div><div class=\"f-desc\">Chips, compute, networking, power, cooling — the substrate everything else runs on</div><div class=\"f-bio\">🧬 The primordial ocean. Source of the FLOPs. The oxygenation event of the AI Cambrian.</div><div class=\"fchips\"><span class=\"fc\">NVIDIA H100/B200</span><span class=\"fc\">AMD MI300</span><span class=\"fc\">TSMC 4nm</span><span class=\"fc\">Google TPUv7</span><span class=\"fc\">Etched Sohu</span><span class=\"fc\">Groq LPU</span></div></div></div>\n        <div class=\"f-layer\" style=\"background:#3D2E20;margin:0 12px;\"><span class=\"f-num\">5</span><div class=\"f-body\"><div class=\"f-title\">Foundation Models</div><div class=\"f-desc\">Pre-trained on trillions of tokens — the body plans every downstream species builds on</div><div class=\"f-bio\">🧬 Body plans. Transformer = arthropod exoskeleton. Post-training stack = vertebrate internal axis.</div><div class=\"fchips\"><span class=\"fc\">OpenAI GPT-5.x</span><span class=\"fc\">Claude 4.x</span><span class=\"fc\">Gemini 3.x</span><span class=\"fc\">Llama 4</span><span class=\"fc\">DeepSeek V3.2</span><span class=\"fc\">Grok 4</span></div></div></div>\n        <div class=\"f-layer\" style=\"background:#5A3D22;margin:0 24px;\"><span class=\"f-num\">4</span><div class=\"f-body\"><div class=\"f-title\">Data &amp; Context</div><div class=\"f-desc\">Embeddings, RAG, vector databases, security — makes AI safe, relevant, and deployable</div><div class=\"f-bio\">🧬 Connective tissue and sensory organs. Environment-specific awareness.</div><div class=\"fchips\"><span class=\"fc\">Scale AI</span><span class=\"fc\">Pinecone</span><span class=\"fc\">Weaviate</span><span class=\"fc\">Hugging Face</span><span class=\"fc\">W&amp;B</span></div></div></div>\n        <div class=\"f-layer\" style=\"background:#7A5228;margin:0 36px;\"><span class=\"f-num\">3</span><div class=\"f-body\"><div class=\"f-title\">Middleware &amp; Orchestration</div><div class=\"f-desc\">MCP, A2A, agent frameworks, observability — where AI becomes a working system</div><div class=\"f-bio\">🧬 Organ systems. MCP = circulatory (model ↔ tools). A2A = nervous (agent ↔ agent).</div><div class=\"fchips\"><span class=\"fc\">Anthropic MCP</span><span class=\"fc\">Google A2A</span><span class=\"fc\">LangGraph</span><span class=\"fc\">LlamaIndex</span><span class=\"fc\">Braintrust</span></div></div></div>\n        <div class=\"f-layer\" style=\"background:#9B6A30;margin:0 48px;\"><span class=\"f-num\">2</span><div class=\"f-body\"><div class=\"f-title\">Applications</div><div class=\"f-desc\">Vertical and horizontal AI products — species adapting to specific niches</div><div class=\"f-bio\">🧬 Species. Some Trilobites (270M-yr dynasty). Some Anomalocaris (apex, then extinct). Some Pikaia.</div><div class=\"fchips\"><span class=\"fc\">Cursor</span><span class=\"fc\">Harvey</span><span class=\"fc\">Abridge</span><span class=\"fc\">Glean</span><span class=\"fc\">Perplexity</span><span class=\"fc\">Sierra</span></div></div></div>\n        <div class=\"f-layer\" style=\"background:#4f74ad;margin:0 60px;border-radius:0 0 5px 5px;\"><span class=\"f-num\">1</span><div class=\"f-body\"><div class=\"f-title\">AI Teammates — The $6 Trillion Opportunity</div><div class=\"f-desc\">Digital co-workers operating autonomously across every role. Language understanding (10×) × autonomous execution (10×) = <strong style=\"color:white\">100× multiplicative force</strong> (Mayfield)</div><div class=\"f-bio\">🧬 The apex organisms — collaborative species replacing and augmenting human labor at scale.</div><div class=\"fchips\"><span class=\"fc\">Sema4.ai</span><span class=\"fc\">Agentforce</span><span class=\"fc\">ServiceNow AI</span><span class=\"fc\">Devin</span></div></div></div>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"fig-cap\">Framework: Navin Chaddha, Mayfield Fund — \"Cognition-as-a-Service\" (Mar 2024) and \"The CaaS Revolution\" (Jun 2025). Biological analogs: author's framing. Company examples illustrative.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <h3 class=\"sub\">Model Release Velocity — The Speciation Rate, Quantified</h3>\n    <p>The best proxy I have for raw speciation rate at the model layer is Anshul Paul's BaseFrame analysis — two charts tracking every user-facing release at Anthropic and OpenAI over the past two years. Anthropic shipped 225. OpenAI shipped 123. The AI Flash Report's industry-wide tally is even starker: on average, a new model now arrives every three days.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"bf-img\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Anthropic shipping speed — 225 releases Jun 2024–Jun 2026\" loading=\"lazy\"/>\n      <div class=\"bf-credit\"><strong>Anthropic's shipping speed, visualized</strong> — 225 tracked releases across Claude apps, API, Claude Code &amp; Cowork, Jun 2024–Jun 2026. Peak week: 15 launches. 2026 already outpaced all of 2025 by mid-year. Source: Anshul Paul / BaseFrame (LinkedIn). Used with attribution.</div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div class=\"bf-img\" style=\"margin-top:16px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"OpenAI shipping speed — 123 releases Jun 2024–Jun 2026\" loading=\"lazy\"/>\n      <div class=\"bf-credit\"><strong>OpenAI's shipping speed, visualized</strong> — 123 releases across ChatGPT, the API, Codex &amp; models, Jun 2024–Jun 2026. Peak week: 10 launches (DevDay Oct 2025 — Sora 2, AgentKit, GPT-5 Pro, Agents SDK in one week). Source: Anshul Paul / BaseFrame (LinkedIn). Used with attribution.</div>\n    </div>\n\n    <p>But Anthropic and OpenAI are only two labs — and BaseFrame counts every user-facing ship, features and connectors included, not just new models. Widen the lens to actual frontier model releases across the eight labs that matter, US and Chinese, closed and open, and the field looks far more crowded, and far more even, than any two-lab chart suggests.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"fig\">\n      <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n        <div class=\"fig-title\">The Speciation Rate, Across the Field</div>\n        <div class=\"fig-sub\">Approximate frontier model releases by lab · Jun 2024 – Jun 2026</div>\n      </div>\n      <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n      <div class=\"bc-wrap\">\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">Anthropic</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\"><div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:100%;background:var(--blue)\"><span>≈13</span></div></div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">OpenAI</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\"><div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:92%;background:var(--blue)\"><span>≈12</span></div></div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">Google DeepMind</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\"><div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:77%;background:var(--blue)\"><span>≈10</span></div></div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">○ Qwen (Alibaba)</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\"><div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:62%;background:var(--fig-warm)\"><span>≈8</span></div></div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">○ Llama (Meta)</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\"><div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:54%;background:var(--blue-light)\"><span>≈7</span></div></div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">○ DeepSeek</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\"><div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:46%;background:var(--fig-warm)\"><span>≈6</span></div></div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">xAI (Grok)</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\"><div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:31%;background:var(--blue)\"><span>≈4</span></div></div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">○ Kimi (Moonshot)</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\"><div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:23%;background:var(--fig-warm)\"><span>≈3</span></div></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"display:flex;gap:18px;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;margin-top:16px;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:10.5px;color:var(--mid);\">\n        <span style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;gap:5px;\"><span style=\"width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:2px;background:var(--blue);display:inline-block;\"></span> US lab</span>\n        <span style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;gap:5px;\"><span style=\"width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:2px;background:var(--fig-warm);display:inline-block;\"></span> Chinese lab</span>\n        <span style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;gap:5px;\"><span style=\"font-size:13px;line-height:1;\">○</span> open-weight</span>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"fig-cap\">Approximate counts of major/frontier model releases per lab, compiled from lab announcements and Epoch AI's notable-model tracking — which logged 87 notable industry releases in 2025 alone. Three things to sit with. First, the gap with the BaseFrame charts above: Anthropic's 225 <em>user-facing</em> releases collapse to roughly 13 actual <em>models</em> — most \"shipping\" is features, not new body plans. Second, the geography is shifting fast: by Stanford HAI's count the US released 50 notable models in 2025 to China's 30, but that gap was 40-to-15 just a year earlier, and China's total releases quintupled from 151 to 849 between 2022 and 2025. Third, on raw artifact volume the ranking inverts entirely: Hugging Face hosts ~2.9M models, the median is just 406M parameters, and roughly half have fewer than 200 downloads while the top 0.01% capture nearly half of all downloads. Qwen alone has spawned 200,000+ tagged variants. Baidu went from 0 to 100+ open repos in 2025; ByteDance and Tencent each ~8–9×.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <p>Here is where most write-ups stop, and where the interesting question actually starts. A chart of release velocity is not self-evidently good news. Cadence is not capability.</p>\n\n    <p>Gary Marcus looked at the same acceleration and read it as the opposite of progress. He called GPT-5 a sign that <em>\"we have reached a point of diminishing returns,\"</em> arguing the version-number treadmill — 5.1, 5.2, Opus 4.5, 4.6, 4.7, 4.8 — is marketing dressed up as advancement. And there is a real point buried in the polemic: when GPT-5.1 shipped roughly three months after GPT-5 and failed to clear it on several benchmarks, the increment looked more like a product decision than a capability jump. Even researchers sympathetic to the labs concede the mechanism — GPT-2 to GPT-3 to GPT-4 were each roughly 100× scale-ups in compute, and the point releases simply are not.</p>\n\n    <p>But the bull reading is just as defensible, and it comes from people who track this for a living. Nathan Lambert, who writes the most rigorous open-model coverage anywhere, reached for the exact same metaphor I am building this series on — he described 2025 as a \"Cambrian Explosion\" of open-weight models with impressive benchmark scores. And he immediately noticed the second half that the venture crowd skips: that open-model adoption is \"brutally concentrated,\" that only the most successful models ever get used at all. His read on the cadence is an \"abundance era\" — a year ago a single delayed release left him with nothing to cover; now there is frontier-grade quality shipping across a dozen labs at once, with Chinese labs matching Western cadence release for release. Clem Delangue, who runs Hugging Face and therefore has the best view of the fossil record anyone could ask for, has been saying the endpoint out loud for two years: eventually there will be \"as many models as code repos.\" On this reading, velocity is not slop. It is the visible exhaust of genuine, multi-front competition.</p>\n\n    <p>Both camps are arguing about the same chart from opposite ends, and the evolutionary frame is what resolves it. Release velocity is the <strong>mutation rate</strong>. A high mutation rate does not imply a high rate of <em>useful</em> mutation — most variants are neutral or harmful, and the Cambrian seafloor was littered with body plans that led nowhere. What velocity actually does is widen the surface that selection acts on. The labs are not all shipping because every release is a breakthrough. They are shipping because in a Red Queen race you sprint just to hold your position — and because every lab knows that the quarter it stops mutating is the quarter it becomes legacy.</p>\n\n    <blockquote class=\"pull\">\n      Most of what is being \"speciated\" is like the <em>Hallucigenia</em>-class. Proto-forms. Experiments. The vast majority of Cambrian body plans went extinct in the Ordovician — not because they were bad, but because the space was too crowded and the environments shifted.\n    </blockquote>\n\n    <h3 class=\"sub\">Pillar 3 — Infrastructure: NVIDIA, the Bitter Lesson, and Etched</h3>\n\n    <div class=\"fig\">\n      <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n        <div class=\"fig-title\">NVIDIA Revenue Ramp</div>\n        <div class=\"fig-sub\">Fiscal years ending January · USD billions · First company in history to cross $5T market cap (Oct 2025)</div>\n      </div>\n      <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n      <div class=\"bc-wrap\">\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">FY24</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\"><div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:28%;background:var(--fig-mid)\"><span>$60.9B</span></div></div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">FY25</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\"><div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:60%;background:var(--blue-light)\"><span>$130.5B &nbsp;(+114%)</span></div></div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">FY26</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\"><div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:100%;background:var(--blue)\"><span>$215.9B &nbsp;(+65%) &nbsp;· &nbsp;$5T mkt cap Oct '25</span></div></div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">Q1 FY27 ann.</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\"><div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:100%;background:var(--fig-dark)\"><span>~$326B annualised &nbsp;· &nbsp;+85% YoY</span></div></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"fig-cap\">Source: NVIDIA 8-K SEC filings. FY = fiscal year ending January. When DeepSeek-R1 dropped on Jan 20, 2025, NVIDIA lost $589B in a single day — the largest single-day market cap loss in US history. That is how leveraged the entire ecosystem is to the substrate.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <p>That single-day number is worth dwelling on, because it is the most important thing the infrastructure layer has told us. On January 20, 2025, a Chinese lab released DeepSeek-R1 — a frontier-grade reasoning model reportedly trained for a few million dollars rather than the usual nine figures. One week later, NVIDIA shed $589B of market value in a single day, the largest one-day wipeout in US market history. Nothing physical had changed. One efficiency mutation, in one organism, made the entire ecosystem briefly doubt how much compute it actually needed — and that doubt alone moved half a trillion dollars. That is the tell: the whole AI phylum is leveraged to a single environmental variable, the economics of compute, and a single mutation can reprice it before lunch.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"callout\">\n      <div class=\"callout-tag\">The Bitter Lesson — and Sutton's Own Second Thoughts</div>\n      <p>The intellectual engine of the entire buildout is Rich Sutton's 2019 essay, arguing that <em>\"general methods that leverage computation are ultimately the most effective.\"</em> The hyperscaler capex chart is that one sentence expressed in dollars — the case for spending on raw compute rather than on cleverness.</p>\n      <p>The irony I can't get past: in 2025, Sutton himself argued that today's LLMs are passive imitators that don't embody the experiential learning his lesson was actually about. The essay most often invoked to justify the spend has been partly disowned — with respect to LLMs — by the man who wrote it. Etched is the purest bet on the other side: a transformer-only chip whose founder, Gavin Uberti, says it plainly — <em>\"If transformers go away, we'll die.\"</em> That is hyper-specialization as an evolutionary gamble, and the Cambrian is full of specialists that owned their niche right up until the environment moved.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <h3 class=\"sub\">Pillar 4 — The Application Layer: Speciation Meets Decimation</h3>\n\n    <p>This is the half of the analogy the metaphor-users skip, so I want to give it the same weight as the explosion. The Cambrian was not just a proliferation event — it was a proliferation event followed by a brutal pruning. And the modern paleobiology, the revised reading rather than the pop-science version, is even more pointed: disparity was maximal early, and most of it was culled, leaving the large morphological gaps we see today. Both phases are running in AI right now, at the same time.</p>\n\n    <p>Peter Walker, who runs the data team at Carta and posts the cleanest startup-mortality charts on LinkedIn, has been documenting the pruning in real time. His read on why 2024 set a shutdown record is worth quoting, because it is pure population biology dressed in venture language: VCs as a class, he argues, did not get better at picking winners in 2021 — so if you fund far more companies at a flat hit rate, you should expect far more shutdowns a few years later. That is exactly what selection looks like when a bloom of new forms meets a fixed carrying capacity. The boom plants the seeds of the cull.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"fig\">\n      <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n        <div class=\"fig-title\">Speciation &amp; Decimation — Simultaneously</div>\n        <div class=\"fig-sub\">The Cambrian had both at once. So does AI, right now.</div>\n      </div>\n      <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n      <div class=\"diptych\">\n        <div class=\"dip spec\">\n          <div class=\"diph\">Speciation — New Forms Appearing</div>\n          <div class=\"di\"><strong>75</strong> new AI unicorns in 2025 — 61% of all new unicorns globally</div>\n          <div class=\"di\"><strong>$37B</strong> enterprise AI spend — tripled in one year</div>\n          <div class=\"di\"><strong>92%</strong> of YC Fall '25 incorporates AI</div>\n          <div class=\"di\"><strong>782</strong> AI M&amp;A deals in 2025 (1.5× prior year)</div>\n          <div class=\"di\"><strong>10+</strong> AI products generating $1B+ ARR</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"dip deci\">\n          <div class=\"diph\">Decimation — The Pruning Underway</div>\n          <div class=\"di\"><strong>966</strong> US startup shutdowns in 2024 (+25.6% YoY)</div>\n          <div class=\"di\"><strong>95%</strong> enterprise AI pilots fail (MIT NANDA, 2025)</div>\n          <div class=\"di\"><strong>&gt;40%</strong> agentic projects canceled by '27 (Gartner)</div>\n          <div class=\"di\"><strong>−81%</strong> collapse in VC fund count since 2022</div>\n          <div class=\"di\"><strong>Jasper, AutoGPT, Builder AI</strong> — already <em>Hallucigenia</em></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div class=\"fig\">\n      <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n        <div class=\"fig-title\">Enterprise AI Spend by Vertical</div>\n        <div class=\"fig-sub\">2025 · USD billions · Total ~$37B · Startups captured 63% of total spend</div>\n      </div>\n      <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n      <div class=\"vgrid\">\n        <div class=\"vc\"><div class=\"vname\">General Copilots</div><div class=\"vspend\">$8.4B</div><div class=\"vcos\">ChatGPT Enterprise, Microsoft Copilot, Gemini Workspace</div></div>\n        <div class=\"vc\"><div class=\"vname\">Coding / Dev Tools</div><div class=\"vspend\">$7.3B</div><div class=\"vcos\">Cursor, GitHub Copilot, Windsurf, Cognition/Devin</div></div>\n        <div class=\"vc\"><div class=\"vname\">Customer Support</div><div class=\"vspend\">$4.2B</div><div class=\"vcos\">Sierra, Decagon, Intercom Fin, Agentforce</div></div>\n        <div class=\"vc\"><div class=\"vname\">Healthcare</div><div class=\"vspend\">$3.5B</div><div class=\"vcos\">Abridge, OpenEvidence, Ambience, Hippocratic AI</div></div>\n        <div class=\"vc\"><div class=\"vname\">Legal</div><div class=\"vspend\">$2.8B</div><div class=\"vcos\">Harvey, Legora, Casetext, Ironclad AI</div></div>\n        <div class=\"vc\"><div class=\"vname\">Enterprise Search</div><div class=\"vspend\">$2.1B</div><div class=\"vcos\">Glean, Guru, Notion AI, Confluence Intelligence</div></div>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"fig-cap\">Source: Menlo Ventures \"State of Generative AI in the Enterprise 2025\" (Dec 9, 2025).</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <p>The two best data sources on whether this spend is real flatly contradict each other, and I think that contradiction is the most honest thing in the whole section. MIT's Project NANDA reported that 95% of enterprise AI pilots deliver no measurable impact on the bottom line. Menlo Ventures, surveying roughly 500 enterprise buyers, reported almost the opposite: 47% of pilots reach production — nearly twice the SaaS norm — with three-quarters of companies now buying AI rather than building it. They cannot both be the headline. But notice that the reconciliation <em>is</em> the analogy: a bifurcation, where a small cohort extracts real value while the long tail stalls. The 5%-versus-95% split is not evidence against decimation. It is decimation, caught mid-act.</p>\n\n    <p>And running underneath all of it is the circular financing that keeps me cautious. NVIDIA invests in OpenAI; OpenAI commits to buy NVIDIA compute. Microsoft and NVIDIA put money into Anthropic while Anthropic commits tens of billions back to compute. Dario Amodei defends the structure as ordinary — one party has chips and capital, the other has confidence in future revenue — and he may well be right. But Michael Burry, the investor who shorted the 2008 housing market, has called NVIDIA the Cisco of this cycle — the picks-and-shovels seller at the center of it all — and the honest test is simple: how much of this revenue originates <em>outside</em> the loop? Vendor financing built the railroads and it built the telecom overbuild. It is not automatically a bubble. It just hides one extremely well.</p>\n\n    <p>Which brings us, finally, to the question that decides who survives the cull: defensibility. The lazy version of the bear case is that most of these companies are \"just GPT wrappers\" — a thin layer of prompt over someone else's model, copyable in a weekend. Andrew Chen at a16z pushed back on this hard, and I think he is right: in a world where the model itself is commoditized, the classic moats — distribution and network effects — come roaring back, hybridized with AI rather than replaced by it. In evolutionary terms, the wrapper question is the body-plan question. A species whose only adaptation is \"has access to GPT\" is sharing that adaptation with ten thousand others; it is plankton. The ones that survive decimation are the ones that fuse into the host organism's workflow so completely that ripping them out becomes surgery. That is not a wrapper. That is an organ.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"end-orn\">· · ·</div>\n\n    <blockquote class=\"pull\">\n      The speciation rate question is answered. The more useful question is not \"how fast are new forms appearing\" — but \"which of these forms are phyla-candidates, and which are going to join the <em>Anomalocaris</em>?\"\n    </blockquote>\n\n    <p>We are still in the Cambrian. The weird wonders are still swimming, the phyla are not yet fixed, and every investor, founder, and new graduate is implicitly answering the same question: which body plan survives the transition to the Ordovician?</p>\n\n    <p>But here is the claim I will actually defend, and it is the one the metaphor-users miss. <strong>AI is exploding in diversity far more than in disparity.</strong> The revised reading of the Cambrian is that the thousands of \"new\" forms were mostly variations on a much smaller set of shared body plans — and that maps almost exactly onto where we are. The thousands of models, wrappers, agents, and fine-tunes are overwhelmingly variations on a single body plan: the transformer. Genuinely different architectures — state-space models, diffusion language models, whatever comes after — remain marginal. Until one of them reaches frontier parity, this is not twenty phyla. It is one phylum, radiating into ten thousand species, all betting collectively that the body plan holds.</p>\n\n    <p>And there is a more hopeful corollary buried in the same revision, which I want to leave you with. When paleontologists went back to the Burgess Shale \"weird wonders,\" they found that most of them were not failed phyla at all — <em>Hallucigenia</em>, reconstructed upside down for years, turned out to be an early relative of the velvet worm; the genuinely novel body plans were far fewer than Gould first thought. The weirdness was real, but most of it was a stem-group member of a lineage that survived. I suspect the same is true of the AI menagerie. A lot of the startups that look like evolutionary dead-ends right now are not doomed novelties — they are early, awkward drafts of categories that endure, and the adaptations that matter will be absorbed into whatever survives, transformed. The species dies; the body plan carries on. That is a gentler verdict than \"ninety-five percent will fail,\" and I think it is the more accurate one.</p>\n\n    <p>That bet is the subject of everything that follows. The next section is about the body plan itself — where it came from, and why it took over the ocean.</p>";
